WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.840
"Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings" is&nbsp;
finally in theaters worldwide. Like any great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.360
Marvel movie, it works on multiple levels. With&nbsp;
that in mind, here are a half-dozen spoiler-heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.320
lines in "Shang-Chi" that mean far more than&nbsp;
you might realize when you first hear them.&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.840
At one point, Jon Jon says "I speak ABC" to Katy,&nbsp;
which many viewers might take as just a cute way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.280
of saying "I speak English." In actuality, "ABC"&nbsp;
is an abbreviation for "American-Born Chinese."&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.800
Writer/director Destin Daniel Cretton&nbsp;
said just before the film's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.720
at a press event attended by Looper,
"Our conversation behind which languages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.280
should be spoken was always rooted in the logic&nbsp;
of the characters. That conversation started in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.200
the writer's room, and then once our actors&nbsp;
came in, it was always a dialogue with them:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.960
Is this character bilingual, trilingual,&nbsp;
quadrilingual? Characters could speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.040
whatever made sense at the time."
As for the ABC line, Simu Liu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.880
who plays Shang-Chi, said the&nbsp;
exchange feels very real, explaining,&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.400
"ABC, of course, means American-born&nbsp;
Chinese. It's the first time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.000
you see in a movie someone just calling&nbsp;
that out, but it's a lived experience."&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.000
Early in the movie, at an unguarded&nbsp;
moment when the characters are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.920
living their everyday lives, we hear,
"We're living in a world where any minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.400
half the population could disappear."
This line not only alludes to the wider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.880
MCU universe, with plotlines revolving&nbsp;
around Thanos and his infamous Snap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.480
but also to the mindset of ordinary humans still&nbsp;
grappling years later with the ramifications.&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.560
Although "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the&nbsp;
Ten Rings" doesn't add any new names to known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.120
victims of the Snap, the event touched everyone,&nbsp;
and it does make clear that it's still hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.440
over the heads of every person on the planet.
The general unease of the populace is backed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.680
up by a support group poster that can be&nbsp;
glimpsed next to the door Shaun enters when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.640
he's visiting Katy's family. It reads:
"Post-Blip anxiety? You are not alone."

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.160
The seemingly straightforward line "Give me the&nbsp;
pendant, and no one gets hurt" represents the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.120
final moment of Shaun's innocence. For more than&nbsp;
a decade, he has been working to build himself a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.920
new life in San Francisco, one far away from his&nbsp;
Chinese upbringing. But as we're about to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.160
he hasn't been neglecting his physical fitness.
When the robber makes that statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.320
Shaun could have just handed over the jade&nbsp;
pendant, shrugged his shoulders and Katy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.560
would likely think no less of him. But that&nbsp;
wouldn't make much of a movie, would it?&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.640
"Does he look like he can fight? Come on, bro."
In a heartbeat, Shaun launches from mild-mannered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.440
bus patron to full-on Shang-Chi, pulling&nbsp;
out acrobatic, environment-embracing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.720
ass-kicking moves that would make&nbsp;
Jackie Chan sit up and applaud.&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.720
In a matter of moments, he doesn't just shatter&nbsp;
half the windows on the bus, but also his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.600
remaining anonymity and any pretense of normalcy&nbsp;
with friend Katy — who watches the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.720
with her jaw open before finally regaining enough&nbsp;
sense to hop behind the wheel and drive the bus.&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.880
By the time the scene is over, everything has&nbsp;
changed. Shaun has revealed himself to Katy as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.680
Shang-Chi, his old life has dragged him back in,&nbsp;
and he must go to China to confront his past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.720
Also, the city of San Francisco&nbsp;
has a really big mess to clean up.&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.760
Movies have employed training sequences for&nbsp;
decades. But it's another thing to show — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.320
explain in raw detail — how day after&nbsp;
day, month after month, year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.400
you were raised to be a killing&nbsp;
machine. At one point, Shang-Chi says,&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.240
"From sun up to sun down, I was taught&nbsp;
every possible way to kill a man."&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.640
Some movies have the hero adept in martial arts&nbsp;
from the get-go, as if it's just something they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.800
picked up; others allude to the training,&nbsp;
but don't truly depict the full dedication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.640
it requires. But by the time you're finished&nbsp;
hearing the tale of Shang-Chi's upbringing under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.680
the tutelage of his father and the mysterious&nbsp;
Death Dealer, you fully realize how he was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.920
to mow down those baddies on the bus.
Keep in mind, for almost the entirety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.880
of "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings,"&nbsp;
Shang-Chi does not have even one ring. In essence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.400
he's an unpowered would-be hero relying solely&nbsp;
on his training to battle a wide array of threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.800
who have everything from super powers to&nbsp;
— at the bare minimum — a machete hand.&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.160
So yes, Shang-Chi is a trained killer, and Simu&nbsp;
Liu is talented enough to sell you on all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.320
years of training. Of course, by the end of the&nbsp;
movie, Shang-Chi does come into possession of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.400
the Ten Rings — so, just imagine how powerful&nbsp;
he'll be the next time we see him in action.&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.840
When Wenwu says "America was terrified of&nbsp;
an orange," some viewers in the audience may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.240
have no clue what the wearer of the Ten Rings is&nbsp;
talking about, but here's what he's referring to.&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.160
Audiences buying tickets to "Iron Man&nbsp;
3" in 2013 thought they were going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.160
to see Tony Stark square off against&nbsp;
arguably his arch enemy in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.920
"The Mandarin" — like the orange. Instead, they&nbsp;
got Guy Pearce's Aldrich Killian as the man behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.200
the man, and Ben Kingsley as Trevor Slattery,&nbsp;
a British actor pretending to be The Mandarin.&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.480
At the time, some loved the bait-and-switch;&nbsp;
others were disappointed or even flat out angry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.120
As the years went by, a third narrative became&nbsp;
the loudest: By teasing a Chinese character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.680
instead giving audiences a white one, Marvel&nbsp;
seemed to be perpetuating a shameful Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.560
tradition of co-opting and whitewashing the&nbsp;
Asian experience. Marvel head honcho Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.280
Feige became determined to not only address&nbsp;
such concerns, but take back The Mandarin.&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:59.680
Which is why, in 2014, Marvel released&nbsp;
a "One-Shot" called "All Hail the King,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.440
which depicted a post-"Iron Man 3" Trevor Slattery&nbsp;
in prison. The short film features a reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.000
telling Slattery that his portrayal has angered&nbsp;
The Ten Rings, the terrorist group associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.120
with the real Mandarin. At the end of the film the&nbsp;
interviewer reveals his true identity as an agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.200
of The Mandarin and takes Slattery prisoner.
"There's someone who wants to meet you."

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:20.880
"Do I know him?"

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.840
"No. But you took his name,&nbsp;
and now he wants it back."&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.880
Which brings us to "Shang-Chi," Wenwu's recounting&nbsp;
of the story, and the later revelation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.040
Slattery has been held prisoner ever since&nbsp;
in the real Mandarin's dungeon basement.&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:40.800
The advice given to Katy when she joins the&nbsp;
battle at Ta-Lo — "If you aim at nothing, you hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.200
nothing" — is more than just "archer mindset."
As we see at the beginning of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.400
Shaun and Katy were leading a largely aimless&nbsp;
existence. Valet parking cars for low wages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.200
spending nights drinking and singing karaoke —&nbsp;
as Katy's family were all too eager to point out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.520
it felt like a roadmap to nowhere. But what&nbsp;
she didn't realize is that her drinking buddy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.600
did in fact have a purpose, even if he&nbsp;
was actively working to suppress it.&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.560
Through Shang-Chi's embracing of his destiny — and&nbsp;
her support as a loyal friend — the two characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.680
ended the film with something to finally aim for:&nbsp;
A future as superheroes, possibly as Avengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.000
and perhaps Katy continuing to embrace her&nbsp;
skills as an archer and car chase driver.&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.680
If you hang around until the very end of the&nbsp;
credits, you'll hear Xialing say, "Let's get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.560
started. We have a lot of work to do," indicating&nbsp;
the dawning of a new day for the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.440
For years, her father Wenwu had forbidden her&nbsp;
from training, forcing the young girl to slyly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.320
study the men around her and resolve to be even&nbsp;
more effective in her movements. Later in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.760
she'd become disappointed by another man, when&nbsp;
brother Shang-Chi left her behind in China.&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.880
Now, after the heroes won the day at Ta-Lo and&nbsp;
her father is no longer a threat to contend with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.480
Shang-Chi has taken the Ten Rings&nbsp;
and once again left — this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.840
for a new life of adventure in San Francisco and&nbsp;
the Sanctum Sanctorum. This leaves Xialing alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.720
to rebuild and rule over a new kingdom, which&nbsp;
is where we find her in this end credits scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.800
surrounded by people training for battle.
Where will this new chapter take her?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.200
It's hard to tell, because in the comics there&nbsp;
is no Xialing, but Xialing could be based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.080
"The Cursed Lotus," a supervillain who's the&nbsp;
older half-sister of Shang-Chi. In the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:20.960
The Cursed Lotus was a gifted hand-to-hand&nbsp;
combatant and seductress who longed to follow in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:26.400
her father's footsteps and led her own criminal&nbsp;
organization called "The Oriental Expeditors."&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.480
Although there's an extremely good chance&nbsp;
that name will not be appearing in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.960
it's possible that the people we see training&nbsp;
under her tutelage are early Expeditors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.200
and as we've already seen from Xialing's&nbsp;
prominence in the underground fighting community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.400
she knows plenty of tough folks&nbsp;
who would likely be for hire.&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.720
We almost certainly haven't seen the&nbsp;
last of Xialing — and Shang-Chi better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.601
be prepared for another smackdown with sis.

